WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:08.700
We’ve all battled traffic as we sludge our way&nbsp;
through a concrete sea on the way to work. This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:15.420
my friend, is the disheartening reality of urban&nbsp;
sprawl. The unbridled expansion of cities has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:20.940
unleashed a slew of nasty consequences for people&nbsp;
and the planet alike. In fact, the UN predicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:29.040
68% of us will reside in cities by 2050. So is it&nbsp;
about time we reviewed how we build those cities?

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:36.060
As concrete jungles invade pristine wildernesses,&nbsp;
precious ecosystems are demolished, resulting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.460
dwindling biodiversity and the demise of countless&nbsp;
species. According to the World Wildlife Fund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.840
habitat loss is the primary peril&nbsp;
for 85% of all threatened species.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.000
Also, cities demand ever more water,&nbsp;
energy, and raw materials to grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.100
which is often correlated with higher&nbsp;
air, water, and soil pollution. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.680
urban air pollution is responsible for&nbsp;
around 240,000 premature deaths in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.720
EU every year. Although it’s interesting to&nbsp;
note that these figures have been falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.160
over the past decade, as many European&nbsp;
cities seek to reduce their emissions.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:16.320
Urban sprawl also gifts us the&nbsp;
joy of lengthier commutes. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.740
forever find ourselves travelling&nbsp;
greater distances to work, school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.800
and elsewhere. This only fuels traffic congestion&nbsp;
and air pollution, tarnishing our overall quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.180
of life. The average American wastes&nbsp;
54 hours per year stuck in traffic.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.280
Urban sprawl also threatens the little&nbsp;
green space that is left within most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.680
cities. Oases like parks and gardens&nbsp;
bestow bountiful benefits upon city folk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:46.440
from mental health boosts to cleaner air and&nbsp;
opportunities for exercise. As cities bloat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.160
these precious places are often&nbsp;
gobbled up by new developments.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.980
After WWI, skyscrapers were hailed as&nbsp;
the remedy to urban sprawl. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.560
rapidly built far and wide, especially in America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.080
However, these towering giants aren’t without&nbsp;
their faults - they’re pricey to maintain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.720
and skyscrapers consume up to 70% more energy&nbsp;
per square meter than low-rise buildings.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:17.100
Skyscrapers are, arguably, unsightly behemoths&nbsp;
that trade beauty for structural integrity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:22.620
maximal floor space. They also cast&nbsp;
long shadows, darkening city spaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:28.140
and they create wind tunnel effects, making&nbsp;
adjacent streets unappealing for pedestrians.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.720
Given these pitfalls, those pencil&nbsp;
fanatics we call architects have begun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.400
exploring alternative solutions.&nbsp;
And some have sought inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.040
from the very depths of the Earth&nbsp;
itself. Enter the Earthscraper.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:52.560
Earthscrapers turn the concept of skyscrapers&nbsp;
on its head - literally. Burrowing deep into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:59.760
ground, they create layers of subterranean spaces&nbsp;
for humans to do whatever weird things humans do.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.720
It's probably dawned on you by&nbsp;
now that Earthscrapers aren't some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:40.500
fresh-out-of-the-oven concept; it's merely&nbsp;
a snazzy term for a very ancient idea.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.800
The Cappadocia region of Turkey, is home&nbsp;
to the underground city of Derinkuyu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.600
built around the 8th century&nbsp;
BC. Whole communities of up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.020
20,000 residents were carved into the pliable&nbsp;
volcanic rock, giving birth to a labyrinth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:04.200
of homes, stables, wineries and chapels,&nbsp;
all nestled beneath the earth's surface.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.480
But now, with new technologies, architects&nbsp;
and engineers can drill ever deeper into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.200
the earth. Building subterranean&nbsp;
megastructures that would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.420
made your average resident of Derinkuyu&nbsp;
choke on his mead. As you can imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:24.840
digging as deep as the Empire State Building&nbsp;
is tall presents a myriad of unique challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.880
that we must overcome. The biggest of which is&nbsp;
making sure it doesn’t collapse in on itself.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.620
To ensure our little earthscaper&nbsp;
Sims don’t get buried alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:39.000
good old reinforced concrete, famed&nbsp;
for its sturdiness and longevity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.340
will be widely employed. But engineers will also&nbsp;
dabble in cutting-edge reinforcement techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:51.000
like soil nailing and deep soil mixing - which&nbsp;
mechanically blends the existing soil with cement.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:55.680
We’re also going to have to funnel plenty&nbsp;
of light down there to ensure a healthy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.880
pleasant living space. Architects&nbsp;
will employ light wells, mirrors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:06.720
and even fibre optic cables to send sunlight&nbsp;
into the structure's depths. Green spaces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.940
and indoor gardens can be added to spruce up&nbsp;
the aesthetics and boost air quality. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.260
growing research that greenery is a crucial&nbsp;
ingredient for maintaining good mental health.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:21.900
Energy efficiency will take centre stage&nbsp;
in Earthscraper designs. By tapping into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.280
geothermal power with heat tubes,&nbsp;
these structures will capitalise on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:29.760
the Earth's consistent temperatures&nbsp;
for heating and cooling purposes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.460
London Underground is an excellent example&nbsp;
of this technology in use - some above-ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:41.280
apartment buildings in London are now being&nbsp;
heated solely by the residual heat collected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:47.460
from underground pipes that run through&nbsp;
London’s tube network. Moreover, water that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:53.040
falls into the Earthscraper can be harvested and&nbsp;
recycled to decrease strain on urban utilities.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:59.940
Speaking of water - you’re probably thinking, what&nbsp;
happens if it floods? After all, we’re essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:07.440
putting a giant fucking bucket into the floor and&nbsp;
hoping it doesn’t fill with water. Fear not, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:14.520
will be gargantuan, advanced drainage systems to&nbsp;
quickly evacuate excess water. But it’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:20.520
groundwater seepage that poses a more significant&nbsp;
threat - that’s water from the surrounding soil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:27.720
making its way in, like rising damp in your home.&nbsp;
For this, architects will implement watertight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:33.000
concrete systems that have been tried and&nbsp;
tested for years in your average swimming pool.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.620
We also shouldn’t ignore earthquakes.&nbsp;
Meticulous structural engineering will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.740
be needed to ensure they endure seismic activity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:47.580
Solutions like base isolation systems and seismic&nbsp;
dampers have been effectively employed in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:52.860
existing buildings, notably across Japan, such&nbsp;
as the Tokyo Metropolitan Government Building.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:58.200
One place that’s genuinely looking&nbsp;
to expand downwards is Mexico City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:04.140
a vast metropolis of almost 9 million people.&nbsp;
Here, traditional solutions have struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:08.220
to keep up with the city's rapid growth.&nbsp;
Especially since the city has put strict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:13.680
height restrictions on new buildings within the&nbsp;
historic centre, so skyscrapers aren’t an option.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:20.040
Enter BNKR Arquitectura, an avant-garde&nbsp;
architectural firm with a revolutionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:27.480
proposal: a 300-meter-deep, inverted&nbsp;
pyramid-shaped Earthscraper, designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:34.020
to provide much-needed residential, commercial,&nbsp;
and cultural space in the heart of Mexico City.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:42.960
The audacious venture aims to tunnel into the&nbsp;
earth right below the city's renowned central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:49.740
square, the Zocalo. By only using the space&nbsp;
beneath the square, the Earthscraper preserves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:56.940
the iconic plaza and offers a resourceful answer&nbsp;
to Mexico City's spatial predicament. The design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:03.000
envisions a sequence of terraced levels plummeting&nbsp;
deep into the Earth, each accommodating a distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:10.380
purpose, such as residential, office, retail,&nbsp;
museums, and various cultural amenities. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:17.400
the suggested design spans a staggering 65&nbsp;
stories. This would be a huge boon to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:23.700
city’s usable space without adding a single inch&nbsp;
to its footprint on the surface of the Earth.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:31.560
However, this concept is not without its hurdles.&nbsp;
Boasting an estimated price tag of $800 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:39.000
the financial feasibility remains uncertain.&nbsp;
And concerns over impacting the historic city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.780
centre have made the locals get&nbsp;
a bit itchy about the project.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:49.620
Besides financial obstacles, there are numerous&nbsp;
engineering and logistical issues to overcome. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:53.400
mentioned earlier, earthquake resistance&nbsp;
is a crucial factor for Earthscrapers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:59.700
and Mexico is especially vulnerable, being&nbsp;
located near 5 tectonic plates. BNKR’s design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:04.260
suggests using a flexible, lightweight&nbsp;
carbon-fibre-reinforced concrete shell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.520
which would supposedly shield&nbsp;
it from seismic vibrations

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:18.000
Even with these hurdles, BNKR Arquitectura's&nbsp;
Earthscraper concept remains an exciting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.200
novel approach to combating&nbsp;
urban sprawl, and I, for one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:27.480
think it would be pretty amazing to see it&nbsp;
come to fruition. And the idea has already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.400
encouraged other architects to think&nbsp;
outside – or rather, beneath – the box.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:39.120
Take, for example, Japan's Shimizu Corporation,&nbsp;
which has proposed an even more daring project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:44.760
called the "Ocean Spiral", a vision of a&nbsp;
self-sustaining, underwater metropolis.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.460
Truthfully, it seems unlikely that Mexico’s&nbsp;
earthscraper will happen in the near future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:58.140
if at all, but there are a few smaller&nbsp;
Earthscraper-esque projects you can actually visit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:05.400
today. Such as Montreal's Underground City, also&nbsp;
known as RÉSO, a sprawling subterranean network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:11.760
spanning over 20 miles. Containing a plethora of&nbsp;
commercial spaces, including shopping centres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:17.580
offices, hotels, museums, and public transit&nbsp;
hubs. The climate-controlled space makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:23.760
a popular destination during the city's frosty&nbsp;
winters, and RÉSO has attracted around 500,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:29.460
daily visitors since its inception in&nbsp;
1962. Proving that Earthscrapers aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:41.160
solely for addressing urban sprawl; they&nbsp;
can be a major tourist attraction too.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:49.200
Skyscraper-mad New York has finally set its eyes&nbsp;
below ground too. The Lowline is an audacious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:55.080
endeavour to morph a forsaken subterranean&nbsp;
trolley terminal into a lively public oasis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.740
This pint-sized earthscraper, would&nbsp;
be outfitted with cutting-edge tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:05.280
like solar collectors and fibre-optic cables,&nbsp;
funnelling sunlight into its depths. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:10.380
after a decade of planning, the project now&nbsp;
looks to be dead due to financial issues.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:16.080
It isn’t just bowling alleys and boutiques&nbsp;
that earthscapers could accommodate. Numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:19.920
industries, like agriculture, could reap&nbsp;
the rewards of subterranean solutions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:25.800
Imagine underground hydroponic farms that&nbsp;
collect and conserve water and offer a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:31.260
precisely-controlled climate for year-round&nbsp;
cultivation. Data centres could exploit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:37.680
Earthscrapers' innate insulation and geothermal&nbsp;
cooling capabilities, cutting down on costs.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:44.640
And, if we do ever get to Mars we might utilise&nbsp;
earthscrapers to create underground habitats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:50.400
shielding us from harsh surface conditions&nbsp;
and cosmic radiation. You could argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:53.820
that building earthscrapers&nbsp;
here on Earth is a priority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.820 --> 00:13:01.080
so we have time to test them out and perfect&nbsp;
the technology before we expand into the stars.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:07.080
In 2017, a team of MIT students won&nbsp;
the international competition "Mars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:11.880
City Design" with their "Redwood Forest"&nbsp;
proposal. It envisioned an interconnected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:16.200
network of earthscrapers on Mars, with&nbsp;
numerous living spaces below ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:20.700
each topped with a large transparent&nbsp;
dome full of lush vegetation.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:28.860
So, as we ponder the particulars of the future&nbsp;
of urban living, let’s not gaze solely skywards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:35.160
but consider the potential lurking beneath our&nbsp;
feet. Because who knows? By trying something new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:38.880
we could reshape our cities for the better. Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:42.720
at the very least, we can build our&nbsp;
own Zion before the machines take over.

